course: dictionary
lesson: 508
title: Many meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word assembly is a good example. «Sam, who hates talking in front of people, spoke up in assembly about the litter in the park.» Sam spoke in front of people, and the class looked at him afterwards, so assembly must be a meeting where the whole class or school gathers together.
explain: «Athens and Iceland's Althing had assemblies much earlier.» Here assemblies are groups of people who meet to make laws and decisions, not a school gathering. Ask yourself what fits: is it a meeting, a group of people, or the act of putting something together? The words around the word point to the answer.
explain: «Assembly instructions show how to build something step by step.» Building a shelf from its parts is the third meaning: putting the parts of something together. When you find a word with more than one meaning, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that makes the most sense.
fact: An assembly is a meeting of the whole school, or several classes together, often in the hall.
fact: An assembly can also be a group of people who meet to make laws or decisions, like the National Assembly.
fact: Assembly can mean putting the parts of something together, like a model or flat-pack furniture.
fact: Useful phrases: school assembly, class assembly, assembly line and assembly point.
fact: In a factory, an assembly line is a row of workers and machines that builds things, such as cars, one step at a time.
exercise: «Sam spoke up in assembly about the litter in the park.» What is an assembly here? | A pile of rubbish left on the ground | *A meeting of the whole school, or several classes, often in the hall | A box of flat-pack furniture | A tidy-up day in the park
exercise: «Assembly instructions show how to build the shelf step by step.» What does assembly mean here? | A loud noise in the hall | *Putting the parts of something together | A group of people who make laws | A meeting of the whole school
exercise: «Athens and Iceland's Althing had assemblies much earlier.» What kind of assembly is this? | *A group of people who met to make laws and decisions | A row of workers building cars | A place where children wait for the bus | A school meeting for songs and stories
test: A class assembly is when pupils perform for their parents. What is a class assembly? | A machine that builds cars | *A performance by one class for parents | A group of people who make laws | A performance by one class for parents, in front of the school
test: In a factory, an assembly line builds cars. What does assembly line mean? | A school hall with rows of chairs | A list of the school's rules | *A row of workers and machines that builds things step by step | A road for cars to drive on
exam: «Hopes are not news: a special guest at assembly does not mean the singer everybody wants.» Which meaning of assembly fits this sentence best? | *A meeting of the whole school, often in the hall | Putting the parts of a model together | A group of people who make laws | A line of cars on a factory floor
